E. Chicago Symphony Orchestra’s Education Center

The hinge work Ellsworth develops in *Places of Learning: Media, Architecture, Pedagogy* is illustrated with a tight, inter-related set of Chicago/New York sites and practices in which a threshold is staged between interior learning space and public exterior and personal and social boundaries are re-worked.

The sustained cases that are worked through the book are:

* U. S. Holocaust Memorial Museum – entry ramp, Hall of Witness, Memorial Garden and the choreographed inside/outside passage between public memory and private mourning;
* Jane Addams Hull-House Museum – the historic settlement house read as a social/architectural hinge between domestic interior and Chicago neighbourhood;
* Manhattan Children’s Museum of Manhattan’s *Art Inside Out* – children’s work placed on the museum façade as a literal inside/outside, adult/child, art/everyday life hinge;
* Anna Deavere Smith’s verbatim/documentary theatre, e.g. *Fires in the Mirror* / *Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992* – used as a performative counterpart to the architectural hinge, the actor’s body holding lived interiority to public address;
* *Bravehearts: Men in Skirts* – the community/performance/video project used to theorise a hinge of gendered embodiment and the boundary between private dress and public display.

Those five items are the hinge set that Ellsworth returns to in the argument. The Chicago Symphony Orchestra’s Education Center is an arts education venue that is cognate with the pedagogical concerns of the book but it is not one of the sites/projects Ellsworth analyses as a pedagogy’s hinge, nor is it developed as a sustained illustrative case in *Places of Learning*. That makes E the clear non-example in the list, with A–D and F being the cases actually used in the text.

